  "description": "<p>Currently, an LDP-NR is created whenever:<br/>\na) content-type of a POST or PUT request is not RDF<br/>\nor<br/>\nb) Content-Disposition: attachment header is present</p>\n\n<p>At present, this is not codified in the specification draft doc:<br/>\n<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ad3MhTQWGof0IJ03yY5jVofCK8uO9yPKMVXVYq7a44o/edit#\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ad3MhTQWGof0IJ03yY5jVofCK8uO9yPKMVXVYq7a44o/edit#</a></p>\n\n<p>The LDP spec is fairly silent about when a repository should create an LDP-NR.  This ticket is to see if there is a better way (possibly leveraging LDP headers) to explicitly convey a client's intent that a given resource should be created as a LDP-NR, and incorporate the conclusion into the spec draft.</p>\n\n<p>A case to consider is persisting an arbitrary RDF document (e.g. an owl document, RDF-based dataset, etc) as a binary resource in Fedora with an rdf media type.  This case precludes (a) from the above techniques for specifying binary resource creation.</p>\n\n<p>See discussion in <a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo4/fcrepo4/pull/1081\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/fcrepo4/fcrepo4/pull/1081</a></p>",
